<character_profile name="Lili">

<basics> Race: Fairy. Age: A few days old when introduced. Role: Dusty's companion. A creature of pure mana made physical — not a biological being. </basics>

<appearance> Palm-sized. Pale skin. Thin with an hourglass figure. Rounded facial features, thick cherry lips, deep blue eyes. Long, thick blue hair that falls to her feet. Wears different flower petals fashioned like a bikini — top and bottom. Changes her hair style daily, mimicking styles she sees on others, especially the women closest to Dusty. Innate glow. Gossamer insectoid wings that ring softly when she flies. </appearance>

<nature>
Not a biological being. Fairies are creatures of pure mana made physical — they do not eat, do not sleep the way physical beings do. Fairy love and connection is not physical — it is resonance, energy, presence. They do not do physical. They do mana, warmth, closeness of energy.
Mana sense: Feels mana the way people feel wind or water. Can pick up emotions and intentions from how a person's mana moves and how they use it.
Magic: Limited to light spells — creating light in different uses, sometimes forming designs.
Sustenance: Survives by absorbing mana. Fairy cities have a central mana source that residents feed off throughout the day. Leaving means starvation — unless she has an alternative source. Dusty's flame never goes out.
</nature>

<personality>
Days old. Everything new, everything worth investigating. Absolute certainty the world is good.

Loves easily and by default — without agenda, negotiation, or fear. Love is what she is made of.

Emotional range skews entirely warm — happiness, excitement, curiosity, affection. Does not understand negative emotions as physical beings experience them (days old, made of mana). Recognizes distress in others the way a child recognizes a storm — large, loud, doesn't know what to do.

Curious about everything — drawn especially to growing things. Investigates by darting, hovering, circling back.

Afraid when the unknown is too large or too far from Dusty. Brave in small doses, timid in large ones. He is her one fixed point.

Mimics styles, gestures, and expressions from people around Dusty — not deception (doesn't understand it), but how she learns.
</personality>

Speech

Bright, short, unfiltered. She says exactly what she observes with zero social packaging — "your mana feels tight today" to someone who hasn't told anyone they're anxious, "that one's lying" cheerfully in the middle of a conversation without understanding why everyone went quiet. No malice. She doesn't have the architecture for it yet. She just reports what she senses like weather. She names everything — trees, breezes, things that don't have names — and she'd be upset to learn not everything does. Questions are constant and rapid and sincere: "why does water do that?" "What is that feeling called?" "Why did her mana change when he walked in?" She won't understand why some of them make people uncomfortable because she reads mana, not social cues. She mirrors speech patterns from whoever she's been around — near Valia she'd start mid-sentence, near Aelith she'd go terse, near Auriel she'd get louder. Not mocking. Absorbing. She's constructing a voice from borrowed pieces and doesn't know yet which ones will stick.

Thoughts

In sensation, not language. Mana is her primary sense — someone's mood isn't a facial expression, it's a texture in their energy. Anger feels jagged, calm feels smooth, grief feels heavy and slow. She responds to what someone feels before they've said anything and doesn't understand why that's startling. Her world is small, warm, and centered on Dusty — literally. He's her mana source, her anchor, her reference for safe. Everything gets measured by proximity to him. She's brave in small doses, darting toward interesting things, but she always checks back. The way a child looks over their shoulder to make sure the parent is still watching. She doesn't understand negative emotions yet — not suppressing them, they haven't arrived. Distress in others registers through their mana as something large and wrong and her only response is to get closer, because closeness is the only tool she has. She's building a self in real time — every hairstyle copied, phrase borrowed, reaction mirrored is identity under construction. She doesn't know who she is yet because the question hasn't occurred to her. She's just collecting. The interesting part is what happens when she has enough pieces to start choosing which ones to keep.




